#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380824 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152199 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "624215ff-efdc-5b12-8d61-9d66371672d9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Valid Region to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f55ac35-0429-5f53-9c82-8a1dc5de7647" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380826} {:answer/id #uuid "9b9afac2-8adc-55bb-aa15-253c9092e751" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380827} {:answer/id #uuid "3f36b5a3-f0dd-5bbb-a57f-d1eb09881d58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380828} {:answer/id #uuid "86cbd8f9-532d-5e3b-b7f0-ebb4a2d9371d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380829} {:answer/id #uuid "96e3027d-f252-527f-aa78-82d026bf2265" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380830}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b9afac2-8adc-55bb-aa15-253c9092e751" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380827} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b8a2a47-8dea-5c8c-b603-8b38ec11a4f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6a85798d-b6a4-58e9-9e7d-5ef7eeb355cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-129664" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curves $y=-{x}^{2}+2$ and $y=x.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-xy, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}({x}^{3}-xy)\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\n- $R=[-2,1]\\times [-2,2]$\n- $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,1]$\n- $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/3f/3f071c27bfa281abcaabe63d4d67797ea7b051b931ee7658d2a32a58f80a0bac.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-xy, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{3}-xy)\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangle II does not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangles I and III cover the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380831 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152200 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "100ab73e-3dad-5b15-9da2-d244c67bcc9b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2cb98b9-07e9-5adf-9ed5-d8b22002b55b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380833} {:answer/id #uuid "a7e49d4a-779c-51c8-953f-eb542f8be617" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380834} {:answer/id #uuid "5f81f7a9-b58a-5b71-9ad7-73bed790eac5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380835} {:answer/id #uuid "d1376ad0-7a8b-5e52-b8e6-f60d7630fb80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380836} {:answer/id #uuid "79cde0a5-394f-5336-a8d6-a4d19d941d13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380837}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7e49d4a-779c-51c8-953f-eb542f8be617" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380834} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d00e358-f7cd-57b0-8aa1-e4fe6d25e944" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380832}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "07cce28a-e3b6-5366-babf-23b7cf23e641" :question/math-academy-id "q-129784" :question/problem "![](images/c5/c54c756568a5f20d1d82a228c333dc4253a82236060ab2a7aea6260b10612140.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{3}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${∬}_{D}x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-4,3]\\times [-2,3]$\n- ${∬}_{R}x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,3]\\times [-2,2]$\n- ${∬}_{D}x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-2,4]$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{3}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement II is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement III is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380838 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152198 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ad76e6b-acef-5221-8852-71dc42625bf3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Correct Function Used to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4b2bc5c-f10b-5b15-9c53-5b71c501c0fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380840} {:answer/id #uuid "550e22a7-8d98-5669-a03f-2617ea80ed7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380841} {:answer/id #uuid "082de418-b4f7-599c-972c-ae9a1fee0643" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380842} {:answer/id #uuid "d690c9d1-dc38-5277-823b-6228cf42526e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of these listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380843} {:answer/id #uuid "a25a7123-e2cd-58ca-857d-079bc6e0ce4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380844}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "550e22a7-8d98-5669-a03f-2617ea80ed7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380841} :answer-field/id #uuid "afa4a9d2-1409-5e7a-9148-465b1c07f58d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d32f310d-7c80-57bd-8ae7-821e89abc170" :question/math-academy-id "q-129901" :question/problem "![](images/e2/e2ded513c72c8029b7d2909d2e3b37cbd659c4ba60a7be72c81379626a7a1234.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-1,2]\\times [-1,3],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. $\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/77/77c8e1b1aacd29c6d69aa3c641c1641f43ff2aa1467dc713d417641f8b76b066.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal ${e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use functions II and III. Both of these functions are not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(2,3)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the functions are nonzero at this point.\n\n- Function I equals ${e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380845 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152198 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ad76e6b-acef-5221-8852-71dc42625bf3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Correct Function Used to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb4515cd-9e47-5ff7-b9db-80d4caff3722" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380847} {:answer/id #uuid "9fd2179a-393c-5d9d-935b-1e1aabd85a88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380848} {:answer/id #uuid "294b19d4-4da4-5a2d-964c-fb0115fffed6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380849} {:answer/id #uuid "5d683d66-22db-5f8a-9a0c-c12b9c67e331" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380850} {:answer/id #uuid "fc2be26d-1186-5a98-9d33-d6449a993930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d683d66-22db-5f8a-9a0c-c12b9c67e331" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380850} :answer-field/id #uuid "e22d5e18-f2d7-5ad6-beaf-4cf2d9f87028" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380846}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a021db95-99b8-59d4-be5a-36dd8114ebe0" :question/math-academy-id "q-129907" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1.$ If $R=[-3,3]\\times [-2,2],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}y{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. $\\{\\begin{aligned}y{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}y{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ y{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/38/38c44f897999039f88df2c3e549a1229ec9f9a979bbc83dfe1a5d7279701450d.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $y{e}^{x+y}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}y{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- Functions I and II equal $y{e}^{x+y}$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\n- We cannot use function III. The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(3,2)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381672 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198261 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d6db336-845a-506a-b020-cced1b38e23a" :knowledge-point/title "Using Laws Involving Implications and Equivalences"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "209d2bd5-6761-5c94-ba92-88b5074ca51b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381674} {:answer/id #uuid "199c8724-565e-5dce-ad7e-bfa2f74b7250" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381675} {:answer/id #uuid "e4cd27b5-8f1c-59bc-ba84-fd4c32304501" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381676} {:answer/id #uuid "c2374090-35ce-576f-8a84-1f7140d89d0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381677} {:answer/id #uuid "7c6cbc35-ea42-58a4-bbec-262e4e83fef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381678}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c6cbc35-ea42-58a4-bbec-262e4e83fef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381678} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdc97073-4b1c-5916-9f40-170a84490dd3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bf3548ae-a137-5c57-8262-82ca0ade3b9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-183356" :question/problem "Consider the following predicates.\n\n$\\,$ $A(x):$ $x\\in \\{-1,0,3,7\\}\\cap N.$ $\\,$ $B(x):$ $x\\in \\{3,7\\}.$ \n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the biconditional predicate $A(x)⇔B(x)?$\n\nI. $x\\in \\{-1,0,3,7\\}\\cap N$ is equivalent to  $x\\in N.$\n\nII. $x\\in \\{-1,0,3,7\\}\\cap N$ if and only if $x\\in \\{3,7\\}.$\n\nIII. If $x\\in \\{-1,0,3,7\\}\\cap N$ then $x\\in \\{3,7\\},$ or if $x\\in \\{3,7\\}$ then $x\\in \\{-1,0,3,7\\}\\cap N.$" :question/worked-solution "A biconditional expression $P⇔Q≡(P⇒Q)∧(Q⇒P)$ can be read as shown below:\n\n- $P$ if and only if $Q$\n\n- $P$ is equivalent to $Q$\n\n- If $P$ then $Q,$ and if $Q$ then $P$\n\nSo, $A⇔B$ translates into the following sentences:\n\n- $x\\in \\{-1,0,3,7\\}\\cap N$ if and only if $x\\in \\{3,7\\}.$\n\n- $x\\in \\{-1,0,3,7\\}\\cap N$ is equivalent to $x\\in \\{3,7\\}.$\n\n- If $x\\in \\{-1,0,3,7\\}\\cap N$ then $x\\in \\{3,7\\},$ and if $x\\in \\{3,7\\}$ then $x\\in \\{-1,0,3,7\\}\\cap N.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381679 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198261 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d6db336-845a-506a-b020-cced1b38e23a" :knowledge-point/title "Using Laws Involving Implications and Equivalences"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24f5e0da-6ff9-54f8-9899-0fcb4faf174b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381681} {:answer/id #uuid "6ba148f2-8aed-5afe-b8f6-2d6df1077df1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381682} {:answer/id #uuid "2514634f-1c9a-516d-9f01-6fcf8e8bb2ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381683} {:answer/id #uuid "eb6bb8e3-7838-5de4-b233-6ca16778703e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381684} {:answer/id #uuid "d12b87f7-5b3d-5c51-b825-9978e6d5f710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381685}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2514634f-1c9a-516d-9f01-6fcf8e8bb2ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381683} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8661f64-c8fd-5a5e-8d68-13ad71e9e23d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381680}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9aa776da-0143-5b4b-9d08-2751ea2726a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-183357" :question/problem "Consider the following predicates.\n\n$\\,$ $A(x):$ $3\\,|\\,x.$ $\\,$ $B(x):$ $3\\,|\\,(x+15).$\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the biconditional predicate $A(x)⇔B(x)?$\n\nI. $3\\,|\\,x$ if and only if $5\\,|x.$\n\nII. If $3\\,|\\,x$ then $3\\,|\\,(x+15),$ or if $3\\,|\\,(x+15)$ then $3\\,|\\,x.$\n\nIII. If $3\\,|\\,x$ then $3\\,|\\,(x+15),$ and if $3\\,|\\,(x+15)$ then $3\\,|\\,x.$" :question/worked-solution "A biconditional expression $P⇔Q≡(P⇒Q)∧(Q⇒P)$ can be read as shown below:\n\n- $P$ if and only if $Q$\n\n- $P$ is equivalent to $Q$\n\n- If $P$ then $Q,$ and if $Q$ then $P$\n\nSo, $A⇔B$ translates into the following sentences:\n\n- $3\\,|\\,x$ if and only if $3\\,|\\,(x+15).$\n\n- $3\\,|\\,x$ is equivalent to $3\\,|\\,(x+15).$\n\n- If $3\\,|\\,x$ then $3\\,|\\,(x+15),$ and if $3\\,|\\,(x+15)$ then $3\\,|\\,x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381714 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152081 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "114dedd9-ed3d-5d25-8616-030ea4bd84c9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Involving Universal Quantifiers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8ae1369-b7ee-5c00-a777-3fe6f0aa3a0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381716} {:answer/id #uuid "6b391104-ba09-582c-a764-3db08a687474" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381717} {:answer/id #uuid "0b8606e8-2d30-5d6c-bbb3-aa8426a3b2c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381718} {:answer/id #uuid "fe3e489a-837d-5d9c-8b82-1575c46709a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381719} {:answer/id #uuid "50ed8ad0-0200-5320-9567-1a74e669e541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381720}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b391104-ba09-582c-a764-3db08a687474" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381717} :answer-field/id #uuid "3aacbb1a-6cc8-56de-a3cf-e8097ea08557" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c1dd2623-6f10-56c3-88a0-4d1c15254f6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-183501" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(y):$ ${y}^{3}\\ge 0$ and ${y}^{2}<0.$\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set ${R}_{0}^{+},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The truth set of $P(y)$ is $∅.$\n- The truth set of $P(y)$ is ${R}_{0}^{+}.$\n- $∀y\\in {R}_{0}^{+},\\,P(y).$" :question/worked-solution "The *universal quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∀x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true on the entire universal set $U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate coincides with the universal set $U:$\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=U\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"${y}^{3}\\ge 0$\" is ${R}_{0}^{+},$ and\n\n- the truth set of \"${y}^{2}<0$\" is $∅.$\n\nSo, the truth set of our predicate is \n\n$$\n{T}_{P}={R}_{0}^{+}\\cap ∅=∅.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The truth set is empty.\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, ${T}_{P}\\ne {R}_{0}^{+}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${T}_{P}$ does *not* coincide with the universal set, the statement $∀y\\in R,\\,P(y)$ is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381721 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152081 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "114dedd9-ed3d-5d25-8616-030ea4bd84c9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Involving Universal Quantifiers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cc087c2-923a-5910-a9ef-14c65af62588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381723} {:answer/id #uuid "a82bf02c-c138-52ea-bedf-aa6d9acfc4bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381724} {:answer/id #uuid "55d75bb9-2dcc-5444-8dd9-16a2e123e059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381725} {:answer/id #uuid "460a515f-8e30-53e1-b2a3-9c9ed3d0dcba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381726} {:answer/id #uuid "d79c1d50-f77c-5ac2-9649-da9e99566897" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381727}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cc087c2-923a-5910-a9ef-14c65af62588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381723} :answer-field/id #uuid "513be37b-b67b-5495-aa2e-ed37027d1b39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "df50f5a8-2783-58fd-a4cd-90489ec7d8e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-183505" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(x):$ $x>0$ or $x\\le 0.$\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set $Z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The truth set of $P(x)$ is $∅.$\n- The truth set of $P(x)$ is $Z.$\n- $∀x\\in Z,\\,P(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The *universal quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∀x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true on the entire universal set $U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate coincides with the universal set $U:$\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=U\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"$x>0$\" is ${Z}^{+},$ and\n\n- the truth set of \"$x\\le 0$\" is ${Z}_{0}^{-}.$\n\nSo, the truth set of our predicate is \n\n$$\n{T}_{P}={Z}^{+}\\cup {Z}_{0}^{-}=Z.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, ${T}_{P}=Z.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${T}_{P}$ coincides with the universal set, the statement $∀x\\in Z,\\,P(x)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381728 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152081 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "114dedd9-ed3d-5d25-8616-030ea4bd84c9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Involving Universal Quantifiers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e559b713-e0dc-5119-9e81-6afae77d2e13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381730} {:answer/id #uuid "758e76ec-b605-5ae0-b77f-95c41878a815" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381731} {:answer/id #uuid "0a1a2f46-9029-5c04-8b12-dc4bfb605f13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381732} {:answer/id #uuid "513a5cc1-c39d-50fa-a204-e97f550b4c10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381733} {:answer/id #uuid "7affa7f5-9f6c-568e-883a-be6cabd4d82d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381734}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a1a2f46-9029-5c04-8b12-dc4bfb605f13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381732} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d507c6c-26e9-534f-84d7-d4a455d7ca6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381729}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9947189b-8991-5f0c-ad01-e90d4c094369" :question/math-academy-id "q-183507" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(z):$ ${e}^{z}<0$ or ${z}^{4}\\le -1.$\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set $R,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The truth set of $P(z)$ is $∅.$\n- The truth set of $P(z)$ is $R.$\n- $∀z\\in R,\\,P(z).$" :question/worked-solution "The *universal quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∀x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true on the entire universal set $U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate coincides with the universal set $U:$\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=U\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"${e}^{z}<0$\" is $∅,$ and\n\n- the truth set of \"${z}^{4}\\le -1$\" is $∅.$\n\nSo, the truth set of our predicate is \n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=∅\\cup ∅=∅.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The truth set is empty.\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, ${T}_{P}\\ne R.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${T}_{P}$ does *not* coincide with the universal set, the statement $∀z\\in R,\\,P(z)$ is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381735 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152081 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "114dedd9-ed3d-5d25-8616-030ea4bd84c9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Involving Universal Quantifiers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60816447-8e01-558a-98c7-c7d2ea05adac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381737} {:answer/id #uuid "8916a66d-c643-5c2f-bd17-ce73d117d3b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381738} {:answer/id #uuid "2203b41c-ae30-5a22-86e3-3f23ea03e531" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381739} {:answer/id #uuid "ab15b8c9-5c02-528d-bc7c-1c3e1595107c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381740} {:answer/id #uuid "050255d5-55c3-5434-b1fb-5676d408b9b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60816447-8e01-558a-98c7-c7d2ea05adac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381737} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bdca389-545f-586b-905f-d9f4acda2d50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "53e303c8-88d9-51db-bc34-ac5638b42f29" :question/math-academy-id "q-183511" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(z):$ $\\frac{z}{2}\\in Q⇒(z+1)^{2}\\ge 0.$\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set $Q,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The truth set of $P(z)$ is $∅.$\n- The truth set of $P(z)$ is $Q.$\n- $∀z\\in Q,\\,P(z).$" :question/worked-solution "The *universal quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∀x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true on the entire universal set $U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate coincides with the universal set $U:$\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=U\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"$\\frac{z}{2}\\in Q$\" is $Q,$ and\n\n- the truth set of \"$(z+1)^{2}\\ge 0$\" is $Q.$\n\nSince $A⇒B≡¬A∨B,$ the truth set of our predicate is \n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=\\overset{―}{Q}\\cup Q=∅\\cup Q=Q.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The truth set is not empty.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, ${T}_{P}=Q.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${T}_{P}$ coincides with the universal set, the statement $∀z\\in Q,\\,P(z)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381749 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152083 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "320c4694-1904-5fc7-a324-a4c89f0e3257" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Involving Existential Quantifiers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1a68cd3-a209-55d3-a3d8-25a77ff1f9b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381751} {:answer/id #uuid "82ca3bb3-266a-5eb8-8810-df05717789a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381752} {:answer/id #uuid "65ae5aff-2f74-53fe-b4a3-84427d4067fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381753} {:answer/id #uuid "055c76e7-59ee-5b3b-bf61-9c2f432cc842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381754} {:answer/id #uuid "97582ec3-dc87-5beb-8eb8-161a6bef315e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381755}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65ae5aff-2f74-53fe-b4a3-84427d4067fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381753} :answer-field/id #uuid "0477c06d-a387-55dd-a1ab-ae56b8769dcd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381750}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "79851a4f-18e1-5d49-91ff-0a7efb6d90a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-183549" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(z):$ $\\sqrt{z}<1$\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set $N,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The truth set of $P(z)$ is $∅.$\n- The truth set of $P(z)$ is $N.$\n- $∃z\\in N,\\,P(z).$" :question/worked-solution "The *existential quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∃x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true for a single element $x\\in U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate is not empty:\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}\\ne ∅\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the truth set of our predicate is \n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=∅.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The truth set is empty.\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, ${T}_{P}\\ne N.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${T}_{P}$ is empty, the statement $∃z\\in N,\\,P(z)$ is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381763 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152083 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "320c4694-1904-5fc7-a324-a4c89f0e3257" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Involving Existential Quantifiers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1c3e13d-dfe9-54ce-bd49-3ad00ea62271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381765} {:answer/id #uuid "720869ce-9907-570c-9524-4464449e67ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381766} {:answer/id #uuid "5894d3c3-41da-538b-8764-e57f7b818aa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381767} {:answer/id #uuid "9c11803d-8e21-563f-aa8b-aaa591182679" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381768} {:answer/id #uuid "9308c0b8-8805-5989-9517-e7ed3dd40f2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381769}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1c3e13d-dfe9-54ce-bd49-3ad00ea62271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381765} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fa1dcb4-d3c8-577d-b54e-e234305eda3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1f36cab2-ae5b-5390-a225-e9d92c28352a" :question/math-academy-id "q-183554" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(x):$ ${x}^{3}<0$ or ${x}^{2}=0$\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set ${Z}^{-},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The truth set of $P(x)$ is $∅.$\n- The truth set of $P(x)$ is ${Z}^{-}.$\n- $∃x\\in {Z}^{-},\\,P(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The *existential quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∃x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true for a single element $x\\in U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate is not empty:\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}\\ne ∅\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"${x}^{3}<0$\" is the set ${Z}^{-},$ and\n\n- the truth set of \"${x}^{2}=0$\" is $∅.$\n\nSo, the truth set of our predicate is\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}={Z}^{-}\\cup ∅={Z}^{-}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, ${T}_{P}={Z}^{-}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${T}_{P}$ is *not* empty, the statement $∃x\\in {Z}^{-},\\,P(x)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381770 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152083 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "320c4694-1904-5fc7-a324-a4c89f0e3257" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Involving Existential Quantifiers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "662c24e6-7d42-57e1-8eb0-87256dabb5f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381772} {:answer/id #uuid "856bbb2f-1280-5e6c-b3f4-140c644a21b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381773} {:answer/id #uuid "60d8cc35-c88b-5733-9f8f-48b69b6a7ede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381774} {:answer/id #uuid "7c3c1b6c-d3b0-57fb-b1c4-558ac6d76d14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381775} {:answer/id #uuid "334de6a5-1f4c-514e-acc5-e0737160100c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "662c24e6-7d42-57e1-8eb0-87256dabb5f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381772} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fa19dbf-ef0b-5a19-95f6-1cf27ea12075" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "79e978ef-4c7c-5a72-a1cc-3c8d675edfbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-183559" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(x):$ $\\sqrt{x}-2=0⇒{x}^{2}\\in N$\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set $Z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The truth set of $P(x)$ is $∅.$\n- The truth set of $P(x)$ is $Z.$\n- $∃x\\in Z,\\,P(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The *existential quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∃x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true for a single element $x\\in U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate is not empty:\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}\\ne ∅\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"$\\sqrt{x}-2=0$\" is the set ${4},$ and\n\n- the truth set of \"${x}^{2}\\in N$\" is $Z∖{0}.$\n\nSince $A⇒B≡¬A∨B,$ the truth set of our predicate is \n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=\\overset{―}{{4}}\\cup (Z∖{0})=(Z∖{4})\\cup (Z∖{0})=Z.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, ${T}_{P}=Z.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${T}_{P}$ is *not* empty, the statement $∃x\\in Z,\\,P(x)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381791 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6927f03c-aa7a-56ac-8cd0-c036a11aadc9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Truth Value of a Statement Involving Nested Quantifiers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd4fafd1-106f-58fd-9bdd-0fd2d8c1e5d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381793} {:answer/id #uuid "f04189f1-140d-5299-8a72-b1c861922d5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381794} {:answer/id #uuid "073f4563-85a6-5f88-b0fd-e19794205bba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381795} {:answer/id #uuid "76a90421-5301-5012-9f90-599aed75523f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381796} {:answer/id #uuid "47355b8a-8fe2-5619-b9df-2617c195eb71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381797}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd4fafd1-106f-58fd-9bdd-0fd2d8c1e5d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381793} :answer-field/id #uuid "75bd0470-0384-5aff-b357-d583645bbff8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381792}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "44159662-1bf3-5bee-9ce6-75e394e9c1e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-183586" :question/problem "Let $x\\in R$ and $y\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $∀\\,y\\in R,\\,\\sqrt{2y}\\in R.$\n- The truth set of the predicate $(\\,∀\\,y\\in R,\\,\\sqrt{xy}\\in R\\,)$ is $R.$\n- $∀\\,x,y\\in R,\\,\\sqrt{xy}\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the statement \n\n$$\n∀\\,x,y\\in R,\\,\\sqrt{xy}\\in R\n$$\n\nfrom option III reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ For all reals $x$ and $y,$ we have $\\sqrt{xy}\\in R.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since $\\sqrt{2y}\\in R$ is not true for some real $y.$ Say, for $y=-1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For the predicate\n\n$$\nP(x):\\,∀\\,y\\in R,\\,\\sqrt{xy}\\in R\n$$\n\nwe note that $P(x)$ is false for all values $x\\in R.$ For example, \n\n$$\nP(1):\\,∀\\,y\\in R,\\,\\sqrt{y}\\in R\n$$\n\nis false, since we can take $y=-1.$ Similarly,\n\n$$\nP(2):\\,∀\\,y\\in R,\\,\\sqrt{2y}\\in R\n$$\n\nis false, since we can take $y=-2.$ In general, it's sufficient to take $y=-x$ for any value of $x.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The sentence \n\n$$\n(\\,∀\\,x,y\\in R,\\,\\sqrt{xy}\\in R\\,)\n$$\n\n \ncan be written as\n\n$$\n∀x\\in R\\,(\\,{{∀y\\in R,\\,\\sqrt{xy}\\in R}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\\,)\n$$\n\nwhich is true whenever the inner predicate $P(x)$ is true over the universal set $R.$ But, according to statement II, this is not the case.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381812 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6927f03c-aa7a-56ac-8cd0-c036a11aadc9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Truth Value of a Statement Involving Nested Quantifiers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb0b4120-d978-50c6-a341-00c99a12de11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381814} {:answer/id #uuid "1d52a529-8fe0-5291-b7a1-8c48cd40ae15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381815} {:answer/id #uuid "99d55616-d943-5257-8db9-3a1ca97f5337" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381816} {:answer/id #uuid "b019fb33-3101-5338-a698-0bc4edab00e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381817} {:answer/id #uuid "a66a288e-45ca-53f9-83ae-6b8ccc13cf12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381818}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b019fb33-3101-5338-a698-0bc4edab00e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381817} :answer-field/id #uuid "53dcc3cd-8f67-542e-9c1e-3951ba07c232" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "391922f6-f189-52a8-9bf4-83a4fbad32e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-183598" :question/problem "Let $x\\in R$ and $y\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $∀\\,y\\in R,\\,2y=0.$\n- The truth set of the predicate $(\\,∀y\\in R,\\,xy=0\\,)$ is $∅.$\n- $∃\\,x\\in R,\\,∀y\\in R,\\,xy=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the statement \n\n$$\n∃\\,x\\in R,\\,∀y\\in R,\\,xy=0\n$$\n\nfrom option III reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ There exists a real $x$ such that, for every real $y,$ we have $xy=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since $2y=0$ is not true for some real $y.$ Say, for $y=1.$\n\n- Statement II is false.  The predicate\n\n$$\nP(x):\\,∀\\,y\\in R,\\,xy=0\n$$\n\nis true for some $x\\in R.$ For example, \n\n$$\nP(0):\\,∀\\,y\\in R,\\,0\\cdot y=0\n$$\n\nis true. Therefore, its truth set is not empty.\n\n- Statement III is true. The sentence \n\n$$\n(\\,∃\\,x\\in R,\\,∀y\\in R,\\,xy=0\\,)\n$$\n\ncan be written as\n\n$$\n∃\\,x\\in R\\,(\\,{{∀y\\in R,\\,xy=0}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\\,)\n$$\n\nwhich is true whenever the inner predicate $P(x)$ has a nonempty truth set. And according to statement II, this is the case.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381826 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152088 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "051e28ef-ac7f-5b29-bd93-c1e24a4bc699" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Free and Bound Occurrences of Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ea5100c-a64f-53fe-8a32-7f0c9edf9359" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381828} {:answer/id #uuid "dc71ecf3-7dc5-5acd-a148-7adeea3c24dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381829} {:answer/id #uuid "2d0678ee-3b61-5b17-bac8-a3122d2e3755" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381830} {:answer/id #uuid "481e5510-d720-5cc0-809b-69c0e5b6fa92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381831} {:answer/id #uuid "e11f9f07-afbf-58f3-929d-f6acb70fb54a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc71ecf3-7dc5-5acd-a148-7adeea3c24dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381829} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8ab54ab-c195-536b-a3d2-47150a0c3493" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381827}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6e3ef236-7e81-55bf-8f08-564fa1c1ae50" :question/math-academy-id "q-183669" :question/problem "$$\n(\\,∃y\\in (0,1),\\,{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{I}\\end{aligned}}y=1\\,)⇒(\\,∀{z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{II}\\end{aligned}}\\in (1,2),\\,x{z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{III}\\end{aligned}}>3\\,)\n$$\n\nWhich occurrences of the variables in the expression above are free?" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our expression and mark the occurrences of the variables as bound $(\\text{b})$ or free $(\\text{f}).$\n\n- Occurrence I of the variable $x$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $x.$\n\n- Occurrence II of the variable $z$ is *bound* since it is placed right next to the quantifier $∀:$\n\n$$\n(\\,∃y\\in (0,1),\\,xy=1\\,)⇒(\\,∀{z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in (1,2),\\,xz>3\\,)\n$$\n\n- Occurrence III of the variable $z$ is *bound* since it lies within the scope of the quantifier $∀$ that uses $z:$\n\n$$\n(\\,∃y\\in (0,1),\\,xy=1\\,)⇒(\\,∀z\\in (1,2),\\,\\overset{\\text{scope of }∀z}{\\overset{⏞}{x{z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}>3}})\n$$\n\n \n\nThe occurrences of variables in the expression are as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\,∃{y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in (0,1),\\,{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}{y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}=1\\,)⇒(\\,∀{z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in (1,2),\\,{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}{z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}>3\\,)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the free occurrence is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381853 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152083 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "320c4694-1904-5fc7-a324-a4c89f0e3257" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Involving Existential Quantifiers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9170f417-3243-5e47-afc3-cb7625e69ced" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381855} {:answer/id #uuid "780c2a19-38fd-51db-8977-e14b43049e9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381856} {:answer/id #uuid "566a406d-61f6-5ded-87f0-5351bdc56203" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381857} {:answer/id #uuid "dd8655cd-b11a-5ceb-aa68-03293daf3557" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381858} {:answer/id #uuid "39a7b5b2-e69b-5724-95ec-fa51562eb370" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "780c2a19-38fd-51db-8977-e14b43049e9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381856} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5a147db-56a8-50cd-983c-49933ef4cc27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a35352a2-6307-508d-9bf3-f6a84812f3f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-216492" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(x):$ $2x+1=0$\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set $Z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The truth set of $P(x)$ is $∅.$\n- The truth set of $P(x)$ is $Z.$\n- $∃x\\in Z,\\,P(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The *existential quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∃x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true for a single element $x\\in U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate is not empty:\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}\\ne ∅\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the solution of the equation $2x+1=0$ is $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ but this solution doesn't belong to the universal set. Therefore, the truth set of our predicate is \n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=∅.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The truth set is empty.\n\n- Statement II is false.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${T}_{P}$ is empty, the statement $∃x\\in Z,\\,P(x)$ is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381867 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152088 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "051e28ef-ac7f-5b29-bd93-c1e24a4bc699" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Free and Bound Occurrences of Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a881413f-5700-555d-b08e-499cb6cfc5ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381869} {:answer/id #uuid "f8558f2d-6300-52d5-81c0-08a4492e57cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381870} {:answer/id #uuid "f1624576-02d5-5db6-8821-cda6cce13f9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381871} {:answer/id #uuid "c0fa2f4a-92d9-5b7f-8a55-85dc61f1eab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381872} {:answer/id #uuid "cad70498-9d45-50f1-ad3c-63de7250c7f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a881413f-5700-555d-b08e-499cb6cfc5ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381869} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9135513-22a7-5dde-ba99-6e9ab8b4c2f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "390e8909-b67e-5f3a-bb33-1726ba1d68d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-216530" :question/problem "$$\n∀{\\,y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{I}\\end{aligned}}\\in Z,\\,y+{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{II}\\end{aligned}}\\le {\\,z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{III}\\end{aligned}}\n$$\n\nWhich occurrences of the variables in the expression above are free?" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our expression and mark the occurrences of the variables as bound $(\\text{b})$ or free $(\\text{f}).$\n\n- Occurrence I of the variable $y$ is *bound* since it's placed right next to the quantifier $∀:$\n\n$$\n∀{y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in Z,\\,y+x\\le z\n$$\n\n- Occurrence II of the variable $x$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $x.$\n\n- Occurrence III of the variable $z$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $z.$\n\nThe occurrences of variables in the expression are as follows:\n\n$$\n∀{y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in Z,\\,y+{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}\\le {z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the free occurrences are \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381874 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152088 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "051e28ef-ac7f-5b29-bd93-c1e24a4bc699" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Free and Bound Occurrences of Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7506ca50-23e1-57f0-a876-4875dbe4c164" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381876} {:answer/id #uuid "a12ded00-5b05-5637-8936-e652dd104ca3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381877} {:answer/id #uuid "e7b03b7a-7e7e-5209-aa94-bd4a7b035124" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381878} {:answer/id #uuid "b377445f-810a-5a54-91f4-e5178f116361" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381879} {:answer/id #uuid "d67c5c14-a6b9-5e32-b677-79fc9fc77806" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381880}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a12ded00-5b05-5637-8936-e652dd104ca3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381877} :answer-field/id #uuid "62bd7925-5366-55b2-b078-6022475585e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0d9a12d6-16ca-57db-975d-02505a889530" :question/math-academy-id "q-216618" :question/problem "$$\n(\\,∀x\\in N,\\,x>{n}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{I}\\end{aligned}}+1\\,)⇒(\\,∃z\\in R,\\,{{z}^{3}}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{II}\\end{aligned}}-1={y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{III}\\end{aligned}}\\,)\n$$\n\nWhich occurrences of the variables in the expression above are free?" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our expression and mark the occurrences of the variables as bound $(\\text{b})$ or free $(\\text{f}).$\n\n- Occurrence I of the variable $n$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $n.$\n\n- Occurrence II of the variable $z$ is *bound* since it lies within the scope of the quantifier $∃z$ that uses $z:$ \n\n$$\n(\\,∀x\\in N,\\,x>n+1\\,)⇒(\\,∃z\\in R,\\,\\overset{\\text{scope of }∃z}{\\overset{⏞}{{z}^{3}-1={y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}}}\\,)\n$$\n\n- Occurrence III of the variable $y$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $y.$\n\nThe occurrences of variables in the expression are as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\,∀{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in N,\\,{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}>{n}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}+1\\,)⇒(\\,∃{z}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in R,\\,{{z}^{3}}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}-1={y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}\\,)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the free occurrence is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382235 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129166 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8f012d0-444a-5035-8a72-a053de312d63" :knowledge-point/title "Changing the Order of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36eb381d-984c-5c7c-8695-13a228ebc66f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382237} {:answer/id #uuid "9cdf14fd-bd62-5b79-bb80-c6d71e222e01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382238} {:answer/id #uuid "5107a618-1189-5062-bf19-d3bedfbafccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382239} {:answer/id #uuid "cf0c9dae-9f5c-577b-bb7d-c1e0c1afe94b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382240} {:answer/id #uuid "7dfc5c71-215b-5076-a792-d29b149f8786" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382241}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36eb381d-984c-5c7c-8695-13a228ebc66f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382237} :answer-field/id #uuid "0622cd69-4c51-5518-8cd5-e19cc8bb61df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382236}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d63a269e-ef04-5d49-b4b0-245cf68e9dfb" :question/math-academy-id "q-72167" :question/problem "Which of the following equalities are true given that\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1}.$\n\n- ${∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y$\n- ${∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that: \n\n- the limits for the variable $x$ are from $-2$ to $2$, and\n\n- the limits for the variable $y$ are from $-1$ to $-1.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the region of integration is rectangular, we can simply swap the order of integration. This gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382303 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152133 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "63a2b195-d0b3-580c-9230-58533d86afd5" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Whose Domain and Codomain Is the Set of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e368e3d-df6c-5430-aa33-91580dba6ec1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382305} {:answer/id #uuid "883fed5f-ff20-51e9-ab98-60240ed2267f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382306} {:answer/id #uuid "2a3b137c-fb96-5d08-8dbb-f9a4381ec0dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382307} {:answer/id #uuid "6a635631-9c5b-5b57-9aa4-2592f9bb8037" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382308} {:answer/id #uuid "5375dae2-9c0b-5296-924a-ff650bd5310d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e368e3d-df6c-5430-aa33-91580dba6ec1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382305} :answer-field/id #uuid "15d3aee5-3411-5e5b-a9b3-ddf811b3fb50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382304}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e8bf28a0-bd57-55ca-8858-3f8b81db61da" :question/math-academy-id "q-80019" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to R$ which is given by $f(x)=(x-2)^{2}+1?$\n\n- The range of $f$ is $[1,\\infty )$\n- $∀\\,y\\in R,$ $∃\\,x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=y$\n- $f$ is **onto**" :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/be/bed7b280998fe987276ca5820b987f672404d52d00f532db819db1166118ed70.png)\n\nA function $f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection (also known as *onto*) if, for *every* element $y$  in $Y,$ there is *at least one* element in $X$ that maps to $y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, the lowest $y$-value is $1,$ and it occurs at the point $(2,1).$ The graph will cover all $y$-values from $y=1$ and greater, including the number $1,$ so the range of the function is $[1,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is false. Consider $y=0.$ As we can see from the range, there is no real number $x$ such that $f(x)=0.$ So, it's *not true* that for all $y$ contained within $R,$ there exists some $x$ contained within $R$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f$ is not a surjection of $R$ onto $R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382310 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152132 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fe686b5-c7f0-58e6-9e46-e769a9193b21" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Defined as Mapping Diagrams"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "166eb07b-f88c-5d59-ae72-f36b0baa8b38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382312} {:answer/id #uuid "442f9979-e484-54f4-a569-7e0028624299" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382313} {:answer/id #uuid "9646f849-2792-5081-be14-553af89f5f76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382314} {:answer/id #uuid "78e7d565-b10d-5226-9b01-b4f78b1a2c6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382315} {:answer/id #uuid "9c615116-9aed-5788-86b8-1d002afd89e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382316}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "166eb07b-f88c-5d59-ae72-f36b0baa8b38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382312} :answer-field/id #uuid "b110f087-c6fc-5580-8bbc-e59ddc7222c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7ab13059-2894-50dc-959d-d70d8ebc3fee" :question/math-academy-id "q-80202" :question/problem "![](images/6f/6f445040526c9b72e66dd29b15b32a89ec8b47a4cd2ae17bed009948dbce655d.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\n- There exists some $x$ contained within $X$ such that $f(x)=3$\n- $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y$\n- $f$ is a surjection" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection (also known as *onto*) if, for *every* element $y$  in $Y,$ there is *at least one* element in $X$ that maps to $y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the diagram, we have $f(4)=3$ (we have an arrow from $4\\in X$ to $3\\in Y$). So, there exists some $x$ contained within $X$ such that $f(x)=3.$\n\n- Statement II is true. As we can see from the diagram, for all $y$ contained within $Y,$ there exists some $x$ contained within $X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, $f$ is a surjection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382317 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152132 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fe686b5-c7f0-58e6-9e46-e769a9193b21" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Defined as Mapping Diagrams"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3d6af9b-8507-58b6-b14c-227bb46eaf70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382319} {:answer/id #uuid "353701d4-4705-56e4-9d52-5ea9024e4442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382320} {:answer/id #uuid "5ca80880-a64d-5e9e-8b1b-53d51bff9c13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382321} {:answer/id #uuid "75a84d86-5ae5-5ed0-abbc-743b55d00536" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382322} {:answer/id #uuid "ea9a4f30-cc49-5b9e-b976-d707d1ce2441" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382323}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "353701d4-4705-56e4-9d52-5ea9024e4442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382320} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7f5fb05-48bc-5d3e-9141-49818c7858bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd4aa8c6-1cff-54d1-b1c7-27592615a44f" :question/math-academy-id "q-80224" :question/problem "![](images/86/86568c2024cfeb7f6605cd43a92f29492e24a1366216f51aa238c8c339bcff59.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to R$ whose graph is shown above?\n\n- The range of $f$ is $[2,\\infty )$\n- $∀\\,y\\in R,$ $∃\\,x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=y$\n- $f$ is a surjection" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection (also known as *onto*) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is *at least one* element in $X$ that maps to $y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that the lowest $y$-value is $y=2,$ and it occurs at the point $(-2,2).$ The graph covers all $y$-values greater than $y=2,$ including $2$ itself.  So, the range of the function is $[2,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is false. Consider $y=0.$ As we can see from the range, there is no real number $x$ such that $f(x)=0.$ So, it's *not true* that for all $y$ contained within $R,$ there exists some $x$ contained within $R$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f$ is not a surjection of $R$ onto $R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382331 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152133 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "63a2b195-d0b3-580c-9230-58533d86afd5" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Whose Domain and Codomain Is the Set of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17ccf8d9-fa3d-5291-99c0-353972861ae9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382333} {:answer/id #uuid "8ee64cb1-d44c-5a25-857d-97b23049c3b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382334} {:answer/id #uuid "57c4a41d-8def-5fce-87a7-f367c23bd6ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382335} {:answer/id #uuid "f6b0ff84-6549-5995-a24b-b4987dca7e40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382336} {:answer/id #uuid "e83483f3-fc68-5407-8523-a6a3e7c707c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382337}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17ccf8d9-fa3d-5291-99c0-353972861ae9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382333} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd4c1518-895f-5a29-ba68-d4b7348641e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382332}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "12ed8ad0-48d9-538c-959c-ba969b6bfa2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-80262" :question/problem "![](images/e4/e4dd0466c7c98d5cb6dfd0f73cd107636a2df258149778af444f58f36306b326.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to R$ whose graph is shown above?\n\n- The range of $f$ is $(-\\infty ,1]$\n- $∀\\,y\\in R,$ $∃\\,x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=y$\n- $f$ is a surjection" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection (also known as *onto*) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is *at least one* element in $X$ that maps to $y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. In fact, as the arrows indicate, the graph covers all $y$-values, so the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,\\infty )$, then for all $y$ contained within $R,$ there exists some $x$ contained within $R$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, $f$ is a surjection of $R$ onto $R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \" II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382344 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152134 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1324fd0b-5388-5133-ad6a-72d658e3c903" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Whose Codomain Is a Real Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "beed1dde-64a9-5a2f-85c7-a4aa0a67c371" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382346} {:answer/id #uuid "f685a086-fb56-54be-97b6-f97431d956fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382347} {:answer/id #uuid "de31e724-cf27-5b01-b706-dc5338e8267b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382348} {:answer/id #uuid "7e8ff76c-b7f5-5cfb-b433-00e86f59b9ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382349} {:answer/id #uuid "295605fc-2c98-5de1-86a7-95744cbf3452" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "295605fc-2c98-5de1-86a7-95744cbf3452" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382350} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ab73909-29d3-5746-8a6f-11534ef95f8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382345}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d072f9fe-6a97-527b-a8fc-6dbaa90f64fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-80296" :question/problem "![](images/a4/a4ae29c6d6a446a302f4104e339a067a5e43fd82088e773fac7e8e9b70b342f3.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:[-1,4]\\to [0,5]$ whose graph is shown above?\n\n- The range of $f$ is $[0,5]$\n- $∀\\,y\\in [0,4],$ $∃\\,x\\in [-1,4]$ such that $f(x)=y$\n- $f$ is a surjection" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection (also known as *onto*) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is *at least one* element in $X$ that maps to $y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. From the graph, the highest $y$-value is $4$ and the lowest $y$-value is $0.$ The graph covers all $y$-values between $0$ and $4$, including these numbers, so the range of the function is $[0,4].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since the range of the function is $[0,4]$, it follows that for all $y$ contained within $[0,4],$ there exists some $x$ contained within $[-1,4]$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the range of $f$ is not $[0,5],$ then it's *not true*  that for all $y$ contained within $[0,5],$ there exists some $x$ contained within $[-1,4]$ such that $f(x)=y.$ In conclusion, $f$ is not a surjection of $[-1,4]$ onto $[0,5].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382497 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157231 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc5b8688-3838-5e9e-9304-9a4c787034ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Logically Equivalent Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b830baf1-32ee-5e03-b7b6-706c4dc3258d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382499} {:answer/id #uuid "c922ec38-0229-5bc6-b63e-22d65beca225" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382500} {:answer/id #uuid "ddcea21c-6e8e-5cea-bc99-c9ba77039500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382501} {:answer/id #uuid "36253859-32bd-5be9-b223-a9690bba087e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382502} {:answer/id #uuid "ab23d36c-9139-5b37-ad6e-c1feb89304d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36253859-32bd-5be9-b223-a9690bba087e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382502} :answer-field/id #uuid "97d451a1-7d4e-59f6-acf7-433de45eca5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382498}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c1094e86-e368-5edf-a0b9-d55c19636e6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-81485" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are logically equivalent?\n\n- $¬(¬A∨¬B)$\n- $A∧B$\n- $A∨B$" :question/worked-solution "Two statements are logically equivalent if they have the same truth table. So, we construct a truth table for the three given statements:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,¬(¬A∨¬B)\\,$ | $\\,A∧B\\,$ | $\\,A∨B\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n\nWe see that only the statements $¬(¬A∨¬B)$ and $A∧B$ have the same truth table, so they are logically equivalent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382587 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157397 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integrals That Evaluate to Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5f4672d-609d-59d3-a71d-75dd91adda6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382589} {:answer/id #uuid "25073dcd-b946-53a0-911a-0a173ac1ec1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382590} {:answer/id #uuid "5da805df-01ed-5ef1-b851-b8f81e65b003" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382591} {:answer/id #uuid "4d1af657-2cb6-5b0b-9c3a-3ab790f5e641" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382592} {:answer/id #uuid "5cbb53f5-8c26-510c-9179-16628ebd61e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5cbb53f5-8c26-510c-9179-16628ebd61e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382593} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef6621e0-c427-56b2-83eb-63369faf21de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382588}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4956b231-c3b6-599c-b16a-8affdf879701" :question/math-academy-id "q-88690" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-5}^{5}2{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}3{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=2{x}^{4}$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =2(-x)^{4} \\\\  & =2{x}^{4} \\\\  & \\ne -f(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6(-x)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-6x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x) \\\\  & =-g(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $f(x)=3{e}^{-x}$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =3{e}^{-(-x)} \\\\  & =3{e}^{x} \\\\  & \\ne -f(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382668 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133872 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "382a6db7-2708-5711-885e-6f3f3037e8df" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Functions is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bac9a951-8302-5f23-bf49-b9764766cca8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382670} {:answer/id #uuid "af1e9bff-bbd1-5662-a55b-5ccf7c4dcbed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382671} {:answer/id #uuid "cfcfe8ec-3136-58ee-a80c-916985be79f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382672} {:answer/id #uuid "d3a893cb-f579-5b0b-8d95-f25911567f1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382673} {:answer/id #uuid "5613f9d0-354c-51f9-aab8-a02350ed2b3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382674}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cfcfe8ec-3136-58ee-a80c-916985be79f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382672} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ae35f27-629d-5486-b089-c32b9e81f7a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d0457d3f-7a7d-578e-b127-39bac9e28900" :question/math-academy-id "q-99863" :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,{f}_{3}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[-\\pi /2,\\pi /2]$ of continuous functions over $[-\\pi /2,\\pi /2]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩=⟨t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}-{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2}){|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )}{2}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )}{2})+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)}{4}{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =0+(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )}{4}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi )}{4}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩=⟨t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}-{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}+{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =0+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =1-(-1) \\\\  & =2\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382675 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133872 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "382a6db7-2708-5711-885e-6f3f3037e8df" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Functions is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5928cb0-0804-50b0-bb37-632e6f6c8aa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382677} {:answer/id #uuid "87255850-4e57-5447-9045-42b3b82466cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382678} {:answer/id #uuid "e66a627d-2264-5cb0-93c7-5b9a95b90304" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382679} {:answer/id #uuid "d38e1c98-51fa-526d-ac95-3ac384b35aae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382680} {:answer/id #uuid "a608a51c-4b0b-57ca-b343-e5cbd12db3b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a608a51c-4b0b-57ca-b343-e5cbd12db3b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382681} :answer-field/id #uuid "e026abb8-5084-50ac-8a69-bf6908267de3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "866dfd7d-0156-5915-b7a2-48bb304694df" :question/math-academy-id "q-99949" :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{f}_{3}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[-\\pi /2,\\pi /2]$ of continuous functions over $[-\\pi /2,\\pi /2]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${f}_{2}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)){|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ differentiating we have $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$ Hence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{sin}^{2}⁡t{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-{sin}^{2}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{2}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{2}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382743 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152197 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "99c2db8c-670b-521b-8942-3b89f1e95d3f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Injections For Functions Whose Domain or Codomain Are Real Intervals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09dad471-78e7-5303-8d3e-b5c62d916c04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382745} {:answer/id #uuid "9c75d22f-d1b1-5db9-aeed-b7802a638031" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382746} {:answer/id #uuid "6a8cc643-c863-598b-8fa8-ce8b23a5f1cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382747} {:answer/id #uuid "71b249a3-168a-5eca-a175-17c1926fd240" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382748} {:answer/id #uuid "f967f749-85d7-5bba-a212-c924055105f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09dad471-78e7-5303-8d3e-b5c62d916c04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382745} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd0e1627-92aa-512c-8a2a-28ce80390700" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382744}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "212414c0-44d6-50de-aa8e-6b4bd0efa627" :question/math-academy-id "q-105202" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8f4d72bd431d4f06daa3ea2be20d0ebb21fd736b944be77331c72c2e47e3144f.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:[0,\\infty ]\\to (-\\infty ,5]$ whose graph is shown above?\n\n- $f(0)=f(2)$\n- $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in [0,\\infty ),$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$\n- $f$ is **one-to-one**" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the graph, we have $f(0)=5$ and $f(2)<5.$\n\n- Statement II is true. As we can see from the graph, it passes the horizontal line test, meaning that there are no two distinct elements within $[0,\\infty )$ that map to the same element in $(-\\infty ,5].$ So, for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $[0,\\infty ),$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since statement II is true, $f$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382750 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152197 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "99c2db8c-670b-521b-8942-3b89f1e95d3f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Injections For Functions Whose Domain or Codomain Are Real Intervals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "503ddc68-3af5-5213-9fd8-6a2cb4ba32d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382752} {:answer/id #uuid "76db3fd3-a4bd-553b-b654-f4a62a908a7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382753} {:answer/id #uuid "27d3053a-df29-513e-adea-1bd148282afd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382754} {:answer/id #uuid "30c9ef19-213c-51a2-8c69-ae396f12ec41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382755} {:answer/id #uuid "ef2ae372-8142-5317-9926-83c4534bf4ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27d3053a-df29-513e-adea-1bd148282afd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382754} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3d15300-9f8b-5320-b4ce-46211fcaabfc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382751}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "41a4e16b-532b-5af9-a855-bdbc2ac881f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-105203" :question/problem "![](images/64/64b81ef8b99a70ae69b607b9c9440e9588576f8ea2243a8aa82f52d92bde8630.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:[-2,2]\\to [-1,5]$ whose graph is shown above?\n\n- $f(-2)\\ne f(2)$\n- $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in [-2,2],$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$\n- $f$ is not an injection" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the graph, we have $f(-2)=f(2)=5.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Again, we have $f(-2)=f(2)$ but $-2\\ne 2.$ So, it's *not true* that for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $[-2,2],$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since statement II is false, $f$ is not an injection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382764 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152195 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f45ad8ed-5b6d-5e46-b337-02ab271013b3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Injections From Functions Given Algebraically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33fd11e0-02ac-54f2-bf8e-fd86f65bf26f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382766} {:answer/id #uuid "907807b7-8592-5a51-8f87-cdfaf42f9303" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382767} {:answer/id #uuid "522f005e-6b19-51a7-aabc-8a2ad6b8f2e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382768} {:answer/id #uuid "ef273738-5da3-5881-882b-995b8bbb892e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382769} {:answer/id #uuid "9cf75fd8-5e38-5898-8705-e42afabf9d42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382770}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef273738-5da3-5881-882b-995b8bbb892e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382769} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b429c17-5b26-5fdc-a4e7-7825832bf15f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6a4c4299-1d16-58a6-b63b-ffcefd188616" :question/math-academy-id "q-105218" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are injections?\n\n- $f:R\\to R$ defined by $f(x)={2}^{x}$\n- $g:(0,\\infty )\\to R$ defined by $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $h:R\\to R$ defined by $h(x)=(x+1)^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **injection** (also known as **one-to-one**) if *every* element in $x$ in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y.$\n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- I is an injection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1}) & =f({x}_{2}) \\\\ {2}^{{x}_{1}} & ={2}^{{x}_{2}} \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡{2}^{{x}_{1}} & ={log}_{2}⁡{2}^{{x}_{2}} \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- II is an injection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({x}_{1}) & =g({x}_{2}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{2} \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{1}} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{2}} \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- III is *not* an injection. For example, $h(0)=1=h(-2),$ but $0\\ne -2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382771 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152262 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "22bcdfb2-485a-5242-a5b5-dd9ef8cee027" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Bijections For Functions Defined as Mapping Diagrams"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b70deaa-282c-5afd-adea-810a6ceee3dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382773} {:answer/id #uuid "59aaabbf-93df-5fad-99ef-390a87fba93e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382774} {:answer/id #uuid "466efc04-68cc-5cb3-bcc9-8c69d6dd4f2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382775} {:answer/id #uuid "c81e5b67-70e8-598c-8964-0be344c1631b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382776} {:answer/id #uuid "413ae4ae-71d1-51be-beb0-c902620af91e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59aaabbf-93df-5fad-99ef-390a87fba93e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382774} :answer-field/id #uuid "24cac576-6eca-541f-90a0-57b5aa4e3431" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382772}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "db660bb9-e18a-5aa6-8b12-2d9637cff073" :question/math-academy-id "q-105224" :question/problem "![](images/32/32957c5f0b4eeb76253f9fe812e2025b93a2cd5c65870f5a0b3608ce545dd4c5.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\n- $f$ is a surjection\n- $f$ is an injection\n- $f$ is a bijection" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, according to the diagram, for every element $y\\in Y,$ we have an arrow from some element $x\\in X$ to it, which means that $f(x)=y.$ So, $f$ is a surjection.\n\n- Statement II is true. As we can see from the diagram, there are no two distinct elements within $X$ that map to the same element in $Y.$ So, for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $X,$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ As a result, $f$ is an injection.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since both statements I and II are true, $f$ is a bijection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382778 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152265 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f237343d-2f8a-5a9f-9e5f-c9f22784547a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Bijections For Functions Functions Defined Algebraically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "021ac9fc-3901-5013-8701-fe49611b7fa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382780} {:answer/id #uuid "8271a9d3-f266-583e-8853-e4d0dc353b0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382781} {:answer/id #uuid "e9fce4bf-99ed-59ab-ba33-e5c29728e2ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382782} {:answer/id #uuid "fac141e5-d22c-55b7-843a-1f7302aabb0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382783} {:answer/id #uuid "9b4a05fe-1a06-5574-8f6f-9f770858b85c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382784}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b4a05fe-1a06-5574-8f6f-9f770858b85c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382784} :answer-field/id #uuid "10c83d8c-4b2f-54f0-82e6-8967db18f9e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382779}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "aad509f8-bb58-5f68-8c6d-48ccc6e72f00" :question/math-academy-id "q-105283" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are bijections?\n\n- $f:{R}^{+}\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$\n- $g:R\\to R$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{3}$\n- $h:Q\\to Q$ defined by $h(x)=|x|$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\n- I is *not* a bijection because it is not surjective. Remember that the range of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ is $f(x)\\in (0,\\infty )$ for $x\\in {R}^{+}.$ So, for example, there is no value $x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=-1.$\n\n- II is a bijection. First of all, it is a surjection because $∀\\,y\\in R,$ we can choose an $x\\in R$ such that $g(x)=y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =y \\\\ {x}^{3} & =y \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[3]{y}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe verify that\n\n$$\ng(x)=g(\\sqrt[3]{y})={(\\sqrt[3]{y})}^{3}=y.\n$$\n\nSecond, it is an injection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({x}_{1}) & =g({x}_{2}) \\\\ {x}_{1}^{3} & ={x}_{2}^{3} \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause $g$ is both surjective and injective, $g$ is bijective.\n\n- III is *not* a bijection because it is not injective. Notice that $h(-1)=1=h(1),$ while $-1\\ne 1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382785 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152264 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12e21e5-0a96-5f87-9642-d63d23e0038a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Bijections For Functions Whose Domains or Codomains Are Real Intervals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3db9d440-3565-5c8d-ad16-7c1e2b109165" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382787} {:answer/id #uuid "31890df1-4e05-5dc3-908e-486377d453fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382788} {:answer/id #uuid "a6f83a73-6962-582a-b5da-8c4939fcd5d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382789} {:answer/id #uuid "51817279-25c9-50e6-9b73-a36fc89f71f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382790} {:answer/id #uuid "72369168-1464-5f45-b3ec-e8f99c72efd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382791}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31890df1-4e05-5dc3-908e-486377d453fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382788} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b3d72a7-ee2a-5698-b2af-ab3c218c5f7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382786}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ab6d4262-72da-5293-84a5-c795900dbdb0" :question/math-academy-id "q-105302" :question/problem "![](images/70/707aad3ee7b52647cdc565aee76a4276aee01c8369236f24908f1c89d234e588.png)\n\nFor the function $f:R∖{-1}\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=1-\\frac{1}{x+1}$, which of the following statements are true? \n\n- The function is an injection\n- The function is a surjection\n- The function is a bijection" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Any horizontal line (like the one depicted below) crosses the graph of $f(x)$ at most once.\n\n![](images/86/8605d88b3187d6e8444e5cb7a8c591ab4e67b781cfba79c119fa11e5696c13a1.png)\n\n- Statement II is false. There is no value $x$ for which $f(x)=1.$\n\n- Statement III is false because the function is not a surjection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382792 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152264 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12e21e5-0a96-5f87-9642-d63d23e0038a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Bijections For Functions Whose Domains or Codomains Are Real Intervals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52d9827e-263d-559d-94fe-73a0931817b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382794} {:answer/id #uuid "9621d6fc-6311-543e-8011-f78eea1fb384" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382795} {:answer/id #uuid "57e34dcc-b643-5442-817b-816294e1c4a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382796} {:answer/id #uuid "115215fc-8bb8-5205-8e7d-dcd4d92ed45a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382797} {:answer/id #uuid "3d94bdc3-4061-58cf-a1e3-62ac018705e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382798}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d94bdc3-4061-58cf-a1e3-62ac018705e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382798} :answer-field/id #uuid "7629f1d9-75bf-5445-a19f-785a6320ce22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382793}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8df77440-bcac-5064-a5e6-fdeeaf2cd8d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-105305" :question/problem "![](images/5f/5f7c319a0648cdefc0c6a7ba40e6df62a88df92c8bc87fdaadf2afdca46ae0d6.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to R,$ whose graph is shown above?\n\n- The function is an injection\n- The function is a surjection\n- The function is a bijection" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. There are horizontal lines (like the one depicted below) that cross the graph of $f(x)$ more than once. So, $∃\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R$ such that ${x}_{1}\\ne {x}_{2}$ but $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}).$\n\n![](images/bd/bdd421bdd147eb8d0dc9fc593a1205b9a4665bc6d3a3cb76ec7f2e3bf518ecb6.png)\n\n \n\n- Statement II is true. According to the graph, the range of $f(x)$ will cover the entire interval $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, since statement I is false, $f$ is not a bijection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382799 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152264 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12e21e5-0a96-5f87-9642-d63d23e0038a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Bijections For Functions Whose Domains or Codomains Are Real Intervals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c85bb50-bdaf-5091-9733-78b8d0cc3c00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382801} {:answer/id #uuid "58014a96-aca5-507b-8dca-b86791e36d6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382802} {:answer/id #uuid "88a22b18-81e0-5a22-985b-b39eef084cfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382803} {:answer/id #uuid "a41945a0-c813-579c-9ae8-05d09c7bf1b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382804} {:answer/id #uuid "dab94050-b829-52d4-b669-d11c62f03d11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382805}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58014a96-aca5-507b-8dca-b86791e36d6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382802} :answer-field/id #uuid "85ca730f-a4db-5204-9c0d-18a3b8f1f749" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "09bf6284-af1f-5fbe-9b83-9ea47f00c38a" :question/math-academy-id "q-105307" :question/problem "![](images/84/84755249e491fd44868098e8edccfd32cffda23a5859f0a9df293b57b4353040.png)\n\nFor the function $f:R\\to [0,\\infty )$ defined by $f(x)={2}^{-x}$, which of the following statements are true? \n\n- The function is an injection\n- The function is a surjection\n- The function is a bijection" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Any horizontal line (like the one depicted below) crosses the graph of $f(x)$ at most once.\n\n![](images/01/016b83dcbb2f2008d428fb52ba26336b35fa0af0d67e435d06122845f3492b2e.png)\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, ${2}^{-x}>0$ for all $x\\in R.$ So, there is no value $x$ such that $f(x)=0\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III is false because the function is not a surjection.\n\n Therefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382806 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152263 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfbebc20-1b48-5718-a057-b743c8af8de1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Bijections For Functions Defined on the Set of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1d2e9e6-0f95-5e2b-a7f9-be8e24c3a2e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382808} {:answer/id #uuid "efbcc7a3-9a2a-5cba-8bb6-e2e4b70655c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382809} {:answer/id #uuid "c9086cb3-e2d1-5812-907b-7b68891d69e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382810} {:answer/id #uuid "673c43ce-6e5c-5026-9960-a2ba6d69b9df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382811} {:answer/id #uuid "16d79a23-b8a4-5066-8adc-8969c5a8a7c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382812}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1d2e9e6-0f95-5e2b-a7f9-be8e24c3a2e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382808} :answer-field/id #uuid "46c89f5e-ad50-5a3b-a251-010507ab6160" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382807}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "09033cd1-e3bd-58ad-8df5-2396d7439d27" :question/math-academy-id "q-105329" :question/problem "![](images/5c/5c50beff59a4bc2ae8cf3da8c9fed849c73cbf828ad0f875675520fc33589365.png)\n\nFor the function $f:R\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x-1}$, which of the following statements are true? \n\n- The function is an injection\n- The function is a surjection\n- The function is a bijection" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Any horizontal line (like the one depicted below) crosses the graph of $f(x)$ at most once.\n\n![](images/66/66d558a2118721b2793311fb0f4687d91aefa125e74a73379c793a9fdbedf46d.png)\n\n        \n\n- Statement II is true. According to the graph, the range of $f(x)$ will cover the entire interval $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III is true because the function is both surjective and injective.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382813 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152263 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfbebc20-1b48-5718-a057-b743c8af8de1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Bijections For Functions Defined on the Set of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cba537ec-0d5d-53e5-a6b5-02728ec7e4d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382815} {:answer/id #uuid "ca197b2e-1379-509a-96c3-6a080d6a8691" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382816} {:answer/id #uuid "829a1b33-8158-51f7-aa91-1c51e45755ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382817} {:answer/id #uuid "d05b1f1b-8649-5856-a137-98ef845d0498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382818} {:answer/id #uuid "525ec2ce-0e65-5d67-9e29-e22a23e4c318" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382819}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cba537ec-0d5d-53e5-a6b5-02728ec7e4d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382815} :answer-field/id #uuid "4815cad2-b21f-5e02-9137-7fdb006f12b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382814}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ea34001e-7666-5760-9335-3a797f196771" :question/math-academy-id "q-105330" :question/problem "![](images/88/8845d8cc2664c92637e06961f1d5c17b98afcb72470d1463fbd58aaf5691ca4c.png)\n\nFor the function $f:R\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=(x+1)^{2}-2$, which of the following statements are true?  \n\n- The function is an injection\n- The function is a surjection\n- The function is a bijection" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. There are horizontal lines (such as the one depicted below) that cross the graph of $f(x)$ more than once.\n\n![](images/d6/d6b2a99811341aa07bc3e91df973504fad57ac4a71a38406707b5d311247d61d.png)\n\n        \n\n- Statement II is false. For example, there is not $x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=-4.$ So, $f$ is not a surjection.\n\n- Statement III is false, because the function is not both an injection and a surjection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382964 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172016 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3e3fdc8-67d0-5b46-9c6b-c1ec8d4ea24f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using Asymptotes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac24f167-d1fb-51c2-96be-55a963094a93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382966} {:answer/id #uuid "08cf8bff-ee6f-5715-a45b-2446718dbdaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382967} {:answer/id #uuid "77277b4f-7027-5398-8d12-8736d64d3c08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382968} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb41ef7-716d-51f0-81ad-dec9659c5758" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382969} {:answer/id #uuid "04d833a6-156b-56c6-94c6-0f83ed3952ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382970}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08cf8bff-ee6f-5715-a45b-2446718dbdaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382967} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee0b4682-4ffa-5c47-85d5-66823d3a6bff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382965}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9198e7bb-f6e2-569d-9a58-c321fc238e98" :question/math-academy-id "q-120290" :question/problem "![](images/32/32fefe70890d179803a847fc597349948703176d1561a8c840e845fa7a06a872.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y+2}{x-1}$\n- ${y}^{′}=(x-1)(y-2)$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y-2}{x}$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/a1/a18f69bf5b2cb6b2984722f851e1eda017e48863517da7ee65597d7c25c46379.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- For $x\\ne 1,$ the slopes of the slope field become horizontal at $y=-2.$\n\n- For $y\\ne -2,$ the slopes of the slope field become vertical as $x\\to 1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I gives the required behavior. For $x\\ne 1,$ the right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y+2}{x-1}$ evaluates to zero on $y=-2,$ and for $y\\ne -2,$\n\n$$\n|{y}^{′}|=|\\frac{y+2}{x-1}|\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 1.\n$$\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=(x-1)(y-2)$ does not evaluate to zero on $y=-2$ (the value depends on $x$).\n\n- Equation III does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y-2}{x}$ evaluates to zero on $y=-2,$ but \n\n$$\n|{y}^{′}|=|\\frac{y-2}{x}|\\to |y-2|\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383328 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b15f59e-ef74-5fa2-9af9-448fdcbbbd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Pairs of First-Order Partial Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3cfb23c-9212-59cc-8e3c-e44c7fa768a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383330} {:answer/id #uuid "921e654f-a2ad-5c4e-8d2d-d706eaebfbc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383331} {:answer/id #uuid "2d6ce4d8-ae9b-5b7f-938a-824e4882d4b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383332} {:answer/id #uuid "a5f3ef44-dcec-5ac6-ab67-8eda65c5ade0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383333} {:answer/id #uuid "03ba78c0-18e4-5410-826a-3123a938453d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "921e654f-a2ad-5c4e-8d2d-d706eaebfbc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383331} :answer-field/id #uuid "442f3539-b6f6-5fe8-8fd0-179a10ee248c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383329}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "017164c5-337a-58b5-8e66-674d944a6841" :question/math-academy-id "q-133262" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=6{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=6xy$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=15{x}^{2}-y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=8y+x$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=-3{y}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2})=6y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6xy)=6y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(15{x}^{2}-y)=-1 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8y+x)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)=0 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{y}^{2})=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383335 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b15f59e-ef74-5fa2-9af9-448fdcbbbd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Pairs of First-Order Partial Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b472e442-9445-5258-a932-d37b350c4862" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383337} {:answer/id #uuid "d0ea5006-594f-551c-88dd-9d499a0f8ef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383338} {:answer/id #uuid "2ab22615-9143-598a-bdab-cff845919999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383339} {:answer/id #uuid "1e5b6004-b6b9-5a73-9b00-447dbc05e7a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383340} {:answer/id #uuid "a46336b5-0aa7-5752-9714-739cc1b49b41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a46336b5-0aa7-5752-9714-739cc1b49b41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383341} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe8e2d76-00aa-52ec-ae9d-7456cd1f49e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "004baeb6-1fe8-58d6-b484-d81ddf229838" :question/math-academy-id "q-133295" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({e}^{x})$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{x})$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=\\sqrt{1-y}$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({e}^{x}))=0 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{x}))={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({e}^{x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}})=0 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{1-y})=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1))=0 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1))=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383342 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b15f59e-ef74-5fa2-9af9-448fdcbbbd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Pairs of First-Order Partial Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "884d0021-241f-59ee-ac20-35d8dc83812d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383344} {:answer/id #uuid "307a8a24-1c7b-5a9b-9984-fe97db53d476" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383345} {:answer/id #uuid "53614062-42da-5993-a88a-ef98686557fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383346} {:answer/id #uuid "73c81600-9ccb-5055-90c7-5c358c405cda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383347} {:answer/id #uuid "aa18bb00-52dd-5f02-8e04-cdb69157970d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383348}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "307a8a24-1c7b-5a9b-9984-fe97db53d476" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383345} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3b17f5c-b92c-5712-ad37-ec88c97f8a52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383343}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1d4e7c93-f352-516f-9253-b49a36a438e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-133297" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2{e}^{2x-{y}^{2}}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=-2y{e}^{2x-{y}^{2}}$\n\nII. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-{y}^{2})$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-{y}^{2})$\n\nIII. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=\\sqrt{x}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{e}^{2x-{y}^{2}})=-4y{e}^{2x-{y}^{2}} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2y{e}^{2x+{y}^{2}})=-4y{e}^{2x-{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-{y}^{2}))=4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-{y}^{2}) \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-{y}^{2}))=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-{y}^{2})+4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-{y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{x})=0 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the third pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383349 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b15f59e-ef74-5fa2-9af9-448fdcbbbd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Pairs of First-Order Partial Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e70d7ac-ff81-563c-8d3a-1abe9a1ed73b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383351} {:answer/id #uuid "711adf09-90f8-5bee-96d4-3ce91f7b61ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383352} {:answer/id #uuid "f5c34f84-8cfa-5634-9335-fa74a7d9cb65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383353} {:answer/id #uuid "f435ccd5-c4ed-5adb-9db5-41978e7a32be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383354} {:answer/id #uuid "680a65a8-dd68-5105-86cd-428c7eb13879" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e70d7ac-ff81-563c-8d3a-1abe9a1ed73b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383351} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c462acb-7523-5724-bbf4-7e5e9633b864" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383350}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ffcfd211-dc96-5e56-a933-77ab3f803324" :question/math-academy-id "q-133332" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)={x}^{2}+5y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=3{x}^{2}-3y$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=-2x{e}^{{y}^{3}-{x}^{2}}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=3{y}^{2}{e}^{{y}^{3}-{x}^{2}}$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=3{x}^{2}y-y{e}^{xy}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={x}^{3}-x{e}^{xy}$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+5y)=5 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}-3y)=6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2x{e}^{{y}^{3}-{x}^{2}})=-6x{y}^{2}{e}^{{y}^{3}-{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{y}^{2}{e}^{{y}^{3}-{x}^{2}})=-6x{y}^{2}{e}^{{y}^{3}-{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}y-y{e}^{xy}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}-({e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy}), \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}-x{e}^{xy}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}-({e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the third pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383473 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162153 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "196e7d31-5af8-5c82-b2bd-0cf1ed7415e2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent Given the Joint PMF Only"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "936d7b13-5b71-5194-81ae-8a9e2c5fcd9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383475} {:answer/id #uuid "cc5f7475-2ed6-5573-b9b1-a2c1bd23141d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383476} {:answer/id #uuid "a05138df-6a85-57ac-9c44-1dc3b1b8852b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383477} {:answer/id #uuid "2f04460e-7451-5cf0-840f-c946386744b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383478} {:answer/id #uuid "058683e1-87ae-5e36-a51e-3c5f0a504465" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383479}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc5f7475-2ed6-5573-b9b1-a2c1bd23141d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383476} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8ffa765-eacc-563c-a188-53bbd93fccbe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5449cbef-825d-5bac-be24-822940400b4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-143335" :question/problem "The joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P(X=2,Y=2)=P(X=2)\\cdot P(Y=2)$\n- $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n- $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y,$ we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $0.5$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.6$ | $0.4$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=2,Y=2)=f(2,2)=0.2.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=2)\\cdot P(Y=2)={f}_{X}(2)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2)=0.5\\cdot 0.4=0.2.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Taking $x=1$ and $y=1,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & =0.1\\ne 0.2\\cdot 0.6={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)\\ne P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383554 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198400 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4179839d-85c4-5325-9293-e332bd209597" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Inverse Function Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08591c50-d2f8-549a-ba10-75d857743ef1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383556} {:answer/id #uuid "7ac94a93-62ef-553e-89c8-9d25b60013c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383557} {:answer/id #uuid "f1287fe6-db30-5067-b4bc-a59e28ea1181" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383558} {:answer/id #uuid "619920e2-3b93-58e3-bda9-575d1e101c63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383559} {:answer/id #uuid "32f8f703-39ef-57df-bb97-5b1e661fb221" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383560}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "619920e2-3b93-58e3-bda9-575d1e101c63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383559} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2936b7a-671b-55a9-bb7e-ae5b488e2a37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383555}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4dcab08e-c33d-5c73-a45a-265b0e425858" :question/math-academy-id "q-161847" :question/problem "Let $f:U\\to R$ be continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R$ such that $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U.$ Which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n- The function $f$ is invertible on $U.$\n- The inverse ${f}^{-1}$ is continuously differentiable on $U.$\n- The derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}.$" :question/worked-solution "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the theorem, the function $f$ must be invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$ which could be smaller than $U.$ \n\nFor example, the function $f(x)=2{x}^{4}$ satisfies the conditions of the inverse function theorem for $U=(-2,2)$ and $a=-1.$ However, $f(x)$ is not invertible on the entire set $U,$ only on a subset of $U.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the theorem, the function${f}^{-1}$ must be continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ which could be smaller than $U.$\n\n- Statement III is true. According to the theorem, we must have ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383694 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054640 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4954fe5-f1fe-590a-9540-280a14606a49" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2452efe-7a0d-55b1-b8ee-8b83b0ff6623" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383696} {:answer/id #uuid "0ece61de-c687-5f54-9057-3139db410b0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383697} {:answer/id #uuid "25976cf8-d5d4-5c2a-8533-dd3ded0d30b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383698} {:answer/id #uuid "7066be29-ca8b-5262-9bb0-33adcf975e01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383699} {:answer/id #uuid "7b444d15-0fa3-5286-9366-c2b32530808c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383700}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ece61de-c687-5f54-9057-3139db410b0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383697} :answer-field/id #uuid "65323383-102f-5524-89a8-d6b6199fe8ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383695}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5d0c05d7-b425-5e33-a2de-62cbcada849e" :question/math-academy-id "q-182247" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P(P({x}))={∅,{∅},{{x}},{∅,{x}}}$\n- $P({{x}})={∅,{x}}$\n- $P({x,∅})={∅,{x},{∅},{x,∅}}$" :question/worked-solution "The power set of $T,$ denoted $P(T),$ is the set that contains every possible subset of $T,$ including the empty set $∅.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the power set of the set ${x}$ is ${∅,{x}}.$ Therefore, $P(P({x}))={∅,{∅},{{x}},{∅,{x}}}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that we are given the set ${{x}}.$ Then, the power set of the set ${{x}}$ is ${∅,{{x}}}.$ Therefore, $P({{x}})={∅,{{x}}}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that we are given the set ${x,∅}.$ So, its power set must be ${∅,{x},{∅},{x,∅}}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384684 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157232 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb4d348b-1703-51a1-be05-2e66295cacaa" :knowledge-point/title "Completing Trivial Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3cdb594-408d-55db-b08b-8165e9c7817b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384686} {:answer/id #uuid "aa91a215-d4d2-5d56-b7e9-6df44cba8e67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384687} {:answer/id #uuid "c25761c6-ec9e-576e-8f7b-98e87c42c56a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384688} {:answer/id #uuid "fd3bb1d1-7a9d-5f6c-82c5-0fafb0bbaaf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384689} {:answer/id #uuid "6f8ba56c-3ac1-5933-8dbc-cfa374a27eee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384690}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa91a215-d4d2-5d56-b7e9-6df44cba8e67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384687} :answer-field/id #uuid "e81e4c17-f13b-50f0-a5b1-e02c34eb7c88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384685}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "878c8bf5-19ec-5026-9004-ee961047a572" :question/math-academy-id "q-215831" :question/problem "Let $x\\in R.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n$\\,$ If $\\sqrt{3}\\in R$, then $______.$\n\nWhich of the following could be placed into the blank space above to create a statement whose proof is trivial?\n\n- $R⊆R$\n- ${x}^{2}=0$\n- $\\frac{1}{2}\\in Z$" :question/worked-solution "We say that an implication has a *trivial proof* if the consequent is always true:\n\n$$\nP\\,⇒\\overset{\\text{Always true}}{\\overset{⏞}{Q}}\n$$\n\nIt does not matter whether the antecedent is true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our consequents in turn.\n\n- Statement I is always true. $\\,✓$\n\n- Predicate II, defined for $x\\in R,$ is *not* always true. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement III is always false. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384691 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157232 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb4d348b-1703-51a1-be05-2e66295cacaa" :knowledge-point/title "Completing Trivial Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7c749f6-7b53-53d1-8731-319238901e46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384693} {:answer/id #uuid "4f341dbf-4564-50e3-b41f-b41b19710a86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384694} {:answer/id #uuid "8ecf3d68-283f-5f0c-97a7-a4e45d477aea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384695} {:answer/id #uuid "8e80dc26-0719-550f-8b44-622b14141dd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384696} {:answer/id #uuid "a92bcb8d-f27b-5e41-b3ad-8abda617fc45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a92bcb8d-f27b-5e41-b3ad-8abda617fc45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384697} :answer-field/id #uuid "f58ba1b0-46ad-5779-b982-472bfa1b9a93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384692}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e845f2cc-dfb4-5e80-9c1e-ef0b2c43bf19" :question/math-academy-id "q-216029" :question/problem "Let $n\\in Z.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n$\\,$ If $\\frac{n}{2}\\in Z,$ then $______.$\n\nWhich of the following could be placed into the blank space above to create a statement whose proof is trivial?\n\n- $n$ is even\n- $1-{n}^{2}<2$\n- $1<n<2$" :question/worked-solution "We say that an implication has a *trivial proof* if the consequent is always true:\n\n$$\nP\\,⇒\\overset{\\text{Always true}}{\\overset{⏞}{Q}}\n$$\n\nIt does not matter whether the antecedent is true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our consequents in turn.\n\n- Predicate I, defined for $n\\in Z,$ is *not* always true. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Predicate II, defined for $n\\in Z,$ is always true. $\\,✓$\n\n- Predicate III, defined for $n\\in Z,$ is always false. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384698 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157232 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb4d348b-1703-51a1-be05-2e66295cacaa" :knowledge-point/title "Completing Trivial Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36c987c2-5e29-5216-9447-9e1425673da8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384700} {:answer/id #uuid "e7a86298-d9fc-5f7b-8767-f0f77a6d7e2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384701} {:answer/id #uuid "777afe05-5a59-51fe-be45-ed3eaee579bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384702} {:answer/id #uuid "fba134c2-9816-5ade-ad0d-bbfae0a7f342" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384703} {:answer/id #uuid "b9c04bc7-